.TH UCNTL 1M "Root Computers Proprietary"
.SH NAME
ucntl \- create/delete/adjust users and groups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ucntl \-a
user group
.br
.B ucntl \-c
user group
.br
.B ucntl \-d
user group
.br
.B ucntl \-e
group
.br
.B ucntl \-g
group
.br
.B ucntl \-m
user shell
.br
.B ucntl \-n
user
.br
.B ucntl \-p
user
.br
.B ucntl \-r
user
.br
.B ucntl \-u
user group pathname
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ucntl
adds and deletes entries to and from the password and group files, and
makes amendments as requested.
.PP
.B ucntl \-g group
adds the specified group to the system. The numerical value of the
group id chosen is the greater of 100 or one more than the highest in
the file.
.PP
.B ucntl \-e group
erases the specified group (which should have no users, and should have
a numeric value greater than or equal to 100) from the system.
.PP
.B ucntl \-a user group
adds the specified user to the specified group.
.PP
.B ucntl \-d user group
deletes the specified user from the specified group. Note that the
specified group must not be the user's default group.
.PP
.B ucntl \-c user group
changes the given user's default group to the specified group. The user
must already be a member of the group.
.PP
.B ucntl \-p user
resets the user's password. This is prompted for in the usual way, but
length checks etc are suppressed.
.PP
.B ucntl \-u user group pathname
creates a new user, with the specified name, in the specified group
(which must already exist) creating the directory specified by
.I pathname
as the home directory of the user. The pathname should normally be given in
full (with initial
.IR / "),"
however the directory may be created as a subdirectory of the current
directory. The mode of the directory is determined by the current value of
.IR umask .
The numeric value of the new user id is the greater of 100 or one more
than the highest in the password file.
.PP
.B ucntl \-r user
removes the specified user (which must have numeric value greater than
or equal to 100) from the system. The home directory is also deleted. This
should normally only be used when the user has been inactive for over a month,
and all files have been copied elsewhere.
.PP
.B ucntl \-m user shell
is used to set the user as 'restricted', i.e. on login a program other
than the shell is invoked, and the home directory is not writable by the
user. The usual shell invoked is
.IR /bin/rsh ,
which for convenience may be abbreviated to
.IR R .
.PP
.B ucntl \-n user
resets a 'restricted' user to normal.
.SH FILES
/etc/passwd		password file
.br
/etc/group		group file
.br
/etc/opasswd		previous password file
.br
/etc/ogroup		previous group file
.br
/etc/ptmp			temporary password file
.br
/etc/gtmp			temporary group file
.SH SEE ALSO
group(5),
passwd(5).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics are reported on the standard error. Non-zero exit codes
denote failure.


